{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 20,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Draper and its Hack the\nMoon initiative for supporting PBS Digital Studios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4800,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These black stones are volcanic rock, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7109,
    "dDurationMs": 4291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is one of the youngest patches of\nland on planet Earth, but that same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11400,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geological event that built this land\nhas provided another window: it allows us"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16650,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to observe a time when the universe was\nstill cooling from the fire of its own formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22160,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And to see this, all we have to do is travel to a telescope on top of the tallest volcano in the world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35200,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, we're driving up to the summit of Mauna Kea on the Big Island of Hawaii."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38140,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the tallest volcano on the planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40940,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At 4,200 meters, the oxygen up here is 60% sea level,\nbut astronomers deal with it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48260,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it is the premier astronomical\nobserving site in the northern hemisphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53200,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To Hawaiians it is a sacred\nsite. And to astronomers,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57900,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's where the Earth meets the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66680,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wow! It's amazing up here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70360,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's like being on another planet. I can already feel the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73470,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effect of the thinner atmosphere. My\nnatural impulse, bizarrely, is to hold my"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78180,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "breath. Must remember to keep breathing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81700,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here we have 13 of the greatest telescopes in the world,\noperated by 11 different countries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87280,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have the Japanese Subaru telescope,\nthe twin Keck domes, over here we have the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92430,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Canada/France/Hawaii telescope, and this is Gemini."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96500,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's where we're going."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98260,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We're here to talk about a very special\nobservation. In the spring of 2017,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103650,
    "dDurationMs": 6850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "astronomers turned Gemini's great mirror\ntowards the constellation of Boötes, the plowman."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110500,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They were looking for a faint\nspeck of light that had been noticed in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114620,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of our great surveys of the sky.\nAstronomers guessed the speck was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a quasar - a vortex of radiant matter\nfalling into a giant black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126620,
    "dDurationMs": 3199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, quasars are the most luminous\nobjects in the universe. What was strange"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129819,
    "dDurationMs": 5581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about this one was its distance. Its\nlight was SO red that astronomers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135400,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "realized that that light must have been\nstretched out – redshifted – by traveling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140860,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many billions of years through our\nexpanding universe. The quasar appeared"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145510,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be more distant than any we had ever\nseen. But that doesn't mean we can't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150610,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unravel their mysteries. And Gemini did\nexactly that. To find out how,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155330,
    "dDurationMs": 3130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we're going to need to go inside."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158460,
    "dDurationMs": 4218,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You've got to see this, it's incredible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162678,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meet the Gemini telescope. This is what a world-class telescope looks like these days."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168518,
    "dDurationMs": 5382,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is enormous. I still remember the first time\nI came to a telescope like this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173900,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It blew me away. Look at the size of this thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176660,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is our window to the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180520,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's cold in here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181570,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They keep the dome at the temperature\nof the upcoming night so that the giant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185650,
    "dDurationMs": 3030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structure doesn't warp and twist with\nthe change in temperature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188680,
    "dDurationMs": 3510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's a little below freezing right now. \nAnd you hear that sound?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192190,
    "dDurationMs": 6930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's the cryogenics. They keep the\nsensitive infrared cameras at 15 above absolute zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199120,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's actually talk about\nlight for a second."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202500,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Light is a wave and the wavelength of that wave determines the properties of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207020,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, visible light – the wavelength range that\nour eyes are sensitive to –"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211360,
    "dDurationMs": 3020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spans only a tiny fraction of the spectrum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214380,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's why we create telescopes – the universe looks"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217340,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very, very different at different\nwavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220400,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, viewed in visible light, the Andromeda galaxy shows us newborn stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225720,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our atmosphere is transparent to visible light, so a ground-based telescope can see a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230950,
    "dDurationMs": 6730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "visible universe, as can we. Gemini is\nbuilt to be sensitive to the infrared."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237680,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The infrared Andromeda is a swirl of\nstar-forming clouds and gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241800,
    "dDurationMs": 4659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some infrared light also makes it through the atmosphere, though it helps to be up here"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246459,
    "dDurationMs": 4861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a mountaintop. Although the air above\nthe observatory is crystal clear,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251320,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it still blurs distant light somewhat.\nTurbulence in the atmosphere causes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255670,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incoming wavefronts of light to be warped,\nand it blurs our view."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260740,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To correct this, Gemini uses adaptive optics.\nIt has a deformable mirror that flexes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266940,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and bends to match and correct the\nwarping of incoming light. To do this in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271900,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "real time, Gemini creates its own\nartificial guide star by shooting lasers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276910,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to twinkle off sodium atoms at 90km height, \nright off the edge of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286340,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the instrument used to\nanalyze the most distant quasar."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290420,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's the Gemini North Infrared Spectrograph – GNIRS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293940,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A spectrograph takes incoming light and breaks it into its component wavelengths, similar to a prism,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300060,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it records how much energy is received at each wavelength. We called that a spectrum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the light analyzed by this\nmachine left its quasar, it was ultraviolet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312320,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But traveling through the\nexpanding universe sapped energy and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316480,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stretched the wavelength of that light\nso that it was infrared by the time it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320860,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reached the earth and this spectrograph.\nTheir redshift tells us how long that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324910,
    "dDurationMs": 7050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light has been traveling – 13.1\nbillion years, meaning the quasar lived"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331960,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when the universe was only 5% its\ncurrent age."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335920,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's a broad blank patch in the quasar's spectrum – it's a stretch of nothing that tells us a ton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343240,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Shortly after the Big Bang, when things had cooled down a bit, the universe was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347590,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "filled with hydrogen gas. It was murky, especially for ultraviolet lights."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353540,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, that gas collapsed into the\nvery first stars, then the very first galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358740,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those stars eventually melted\naway the remaining hydrogen in a process"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363250,
    "dDurationMs": 4508,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called reionization, leaving a\ncrystal-clear universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367758,
    "dDurationMs": 7302,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this quasar shines out from the era of those first stars before they'd finished the job of reionization."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375060,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Much of the quasars once ultraviolet light was sucked up before it escaped the early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381140,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And what about the supermassive black hole at the center of the quasar?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The same signature wavelengths used to measure redshift are also broadened"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389600,
    "dDurationMs": 4825,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to the extreme speeds of matter moving near the black hole. That allows us to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394425,
    "dDurationMs": 2595,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "estimate the mass of the black hole:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397020,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "800 million Suns."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399360,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it replaced our Sun, it would easily swallow Saturn's orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403620,
    "dDurationMs": 3130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Scientists struggled to\nfigure out how it could grow to that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406750,
    "dDurationMs": 6810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "insane size in a tiny fraction of the\nage of the universe. We are expanding our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413560,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understanding of physics to figure this\none out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416620,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That tiny speck is both a revelation and a mystery. It literally shines a light on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422220,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earliest epochs of our universe, teaching us about our most fundamental origins."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427880,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it also opens new questions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430520,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And our great telescopes – our portals to the universe past and present –"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435160,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will tackle those questions too\nand ultimately bring us closer to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439420,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understanding this mysterious, this\nmagnificent space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447240,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to advances in our understanding of general\nrelativity and some mind-blowing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451000,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advances in technology, there are other\nways humanity can see the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455080,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beyond the electromagnetic spectrum that\nwe observe with traditional telescopes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458770,
    "dDurationMs": 3590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can now see ripples in the fabric of\nspace time itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463180,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Look out for Physics Girl's exploration of gravitational waves at LIGO."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467600,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Draper and its Hack the Moon initiative for supporting PBS Digital Studios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472460,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You know the story of the astronauts who landed on the Moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475280,
    "dDurationMs": 2210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now you can visit WeHackTheMoon.com"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477490,
    "dDurationMs": 6630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to discover the story of the male and\nfemale engineers who guided them there and back safely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484120,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hack the Moon chronicles the engineers and technologies behind the Apollo missions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488740,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brought to you by Draper, the site is\nfull of images, videos, and stories about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493480,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the people who hacked the moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496040,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "PBS is bringing you the universe with the\n SUMMER OF SPACE,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499700,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which includes six\nincredible new science and history shows"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502690,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "airing on PBS and streaming on pbs.org\nand the PBS video app."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506690,
    "dDurationMs": 7210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Watch it all on PBS.org/SummerOfSpace."
    } ]
  } ]
}
